    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d855">

                <pb facs="0810" n="800" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg800"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.48/2189: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-10-23T11:00:00+09:00">October 23,
                            1941—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:12 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1675. Embassy’s 1414, September 7, 9 a.m.,<anchor n="38" xml:id="d855-f1"/>
                    Japanese seizure of Red Cross cracked wheat Canton. A reply dated October 21 to
                    the Embassy’s representations has been received from the Foreign Office stating
                    that the wheat was seized by the Japanese Navy while being smuggled from Hong
                    Kong in violation of the Japanese blockade, a fact which is recognized by
                    persons connected with the Canton Relief Society, presumably a Japanese
                    sponsored organization.</p>
                <p>There is no denial that the wheat was sold commercially.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department via Shanghai, Chungking, Peiping, Hong Kong, Canton.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
